The '''Kott''' ('''Kot''') language ({{langx|ru|Коттский язык}}) is an extinct [[Yeniseian languages|Yeniseian language]] that was formerly spoken in central [[Siberia]] by the banks of the [[Mana (Russia)|Mana River]], a tributary of the [[Yenisei river]].  It became extinct in the 1850s. Kott was closely related to [[Ket language|Ket]], still spoken farther north along the Yenisei river. [[Assan language|Assan]], a close relative, is sometimes considered a dialect of Kott.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Werner |first=Heinrich |title=Abriß der kottischen Grammatik |date=1997 |publisher=Harrassowitz |isbn=3-447-03971-X |location=Wiesbaden |pages=13 |language=de |trans-title=Kott Grammar Outline}}</ref> The term ''kott'' may be derived from [[Buryat language|Buryat]] ''{{Langx|bua|qota|label=none}}'' 'town', applied to neighbouring non-pastoral peoples, including the last few Kotts.<ref name=":2">{{Citation |last=Vajda |first=Edward |title=8 The Yeniseian language family |date=2024-02-19 |work=The Languages and Linguistics of Northern Asia |pages=365–480 |editor-last=Vajda |editor-first=Edward |url=https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110556216-008/html |access-date=2024-06-26 |publisher=De Gruyter |doi=10.1515/9783110556216-008 |isbn=978-3-11-055621-6|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== Geographical distribution ==
Kott was spoken to the southeast of [[Krasnoyarsk]], in the [[Biryusa]] and [[Kan (river)|Kan]] river basins. However, [[hydronym]]s indicate a much wider area in the past, ranging from the [[Uda (Selenga)|Uda]] and [[Chuna (river)|Chuna]] rivers in the east to the [[Tom (river)|Tom]] in the west.<ref>{{Cite web |title=КОТТСКИЙ ЯЗЫК • Большая российская энциклопедия - электронная версия |url=https://old.bigenc.ru/linguistics/text/2104056 |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=old.bigenc.ru}}</ref>

== Documentation ==
One of the earliest written records of Kott is in 1791, with the publication of [[Peter Simon Pallas]]'s ''{{Langx|ru|Сравнительный словарь всѣхъ языковъ и нарѣчій, по азбучному порядку расположенный|label=none}}'', a comparative dictionary of various world languages and dialects. In 1858, [[Matthias Castrén]] published the grammar and dictionary (''Versuch einer jenissei-ostjakischen und kottischen Sprachlehre''), which included material on the Kott and Ket ([[Yenisei-Ostyak]]) languages, recording two different dialects of Kott in the 1840s.<ref name="Georg" /> There also exists two books written by [[Heinrich Werner (linguist)|Heinrich Werner]] about the Kott language, namely ''{{Langx|ru|Коттский язык|label=none}}'' (Kottskij jazyk), which includes a 110-page Russian-Kott glossary,<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Verner |first=G. K. (Г. К. Вернер) |title=Kottsky yazyk |date=1990 |publisher=Izdatel'stvo rostovskogo universiteta |isbn=5-7507-0357-6 |location=Rostov-na-Donu |language=ru |script-title=ru:Коттский язык |trans-title=Kott Language}}</ref> and {{Langx|de|Abriß der kottischen Grammatik|label=none}}.<ref name=":0" />

